 # Dynamic Scene and Temporal Modeling
 
-Also called: Dynamic NeRF, 4D Radiance Fields, Temporal NeRF
+Also called: Dynamic NeRF, 4D NeRF, Temporal NeRF
 
 ## Overview
-Dynamic Scene and Temporal Modeling adapts Neural Radiance Fields to handle time-varying scenes, solving the limitation of static scene representation in video sequences [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. It enables the reconstruction and novel view synthesis of dynamic content by allowing density and color fields to vary over time [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. Unlike static NeRF, which assumes a fixed geometry, these methods explicitly model temporal dynamics through various mechanisms such as direct time encoding, deformation fields, particle systems, or semantic stratification [13848-gear-nerf.md][14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md][15541-forward-warping-for-novel-view-synthesis.md][15550-dynamic-appearance-particle-neural-radiance-field.md][15551-kfd-nerf.md].
+Dynamic Scene and Temporal Modeling extends static Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to handle video sequences and time-varying scenes by introducing a temporal dimension $t$ into the radiance field representation [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. It solves the problem of reconstructing dynamic content, which standard NeRF cannot represent, by allowing density and color to vary over time [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. Unlike static methods, it explicitly models scene dynamics, either through direct time conditioning, deformation fields, or particle systems [13848-gear-nerf.md][15541-forward-warping-for-novel-view-synthesis.md][15550-dynamic-appearance-particle-neural-radiance-field.md][15551-kfd-nerf.md].
 
 ## Shared principles
-The standard NeRF architecture [Parent](3065-nerf-hyperparameters.md) serves as the baseline. Modifications generally fall into three categories [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md]:
-1.  **Input Expansion:** Adding new conditioning vectors (e.g., time $t$, appearance embeddings $\mathbf{z}_i$, or fluid volume $v$) to the input concatenation [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
-2.  **Output Expansion:** Adding auxiliary heads to predict values other than $\sigma$ and $\mathbf{c}$, such as semantic labels $y_{aux}$, object codes $o$, or area encoding probabilities $p$. These are rendered using the same transmittance weights $T_i$ and opacity $\alpha_i$ as color:
-    $$
-    \hat{Y}(r) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} T_i \alpha_i y_{aux}(x_i)
-    $$
-    [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
-3.  **Representation Substitution:** Replacing the continuous MLP function with discrete structures (voxel grids, hash tables) or different implicit surfaces (SDFs) [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
+The standard NeRF architecture [Parent](3065-nerf-hyperparameters.md) serves as the baseline. The primary modification is the input expansion to include time $t$ alongside spatial position $\mathbf{x}$ and view direction $\mathbf{d}$ [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. The volume rendering equation is adapted to integrate over time-dependent density $\sigma(\mathbf{x}, t)$ and color $\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}, t)$ [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]:
 
-The volume rendering equation is adapted to include time $t$ [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]:
 $$
 C(\mathbf{r}, t) = \int_{s_n}^{s_f} T(s, t) \sigma(\mathbf{r}(s), t) \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r}(s), \mathbf{d}, t) ds
 $$
-where $\mathbf{r}(s) = \mathbf{o} + s\mathbf{d}$ is the ray equation, and $\sigma$ and $\mathbf{c}$ are now functions of space, view direction, and time [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md].
+
+where $\mathbf{r}(s) = \mathbf{o} + s\mathbf{d}$ is the ray equation and $T(s, t)$ is the accumulated transmittance at time $t$ [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. Time $t$ is typically processed using positional encoding $\gamma_t(t)$ similar to spatial coordinates [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Gear-NeRF](13848-gear-nerf.md)
-Stratifies a 4D scene into multiple "gear" levels based on semantic motion scales, allowing for region-specific spatio-temporal sampling resolution. It solves the inefficiency of uniform 4D sampling by assigning higher-resolution feature volumes to regions with larger motion, while sharing static spatial features across all gears. Unlike [NeRF with Time Parameter](14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md) which treats time as a continuous input to a single network, Gear-NeRF uses discrete gear levels to allocate computational resources proportional to motion intensity, and unlike [KFD-NeRF](15551-kfd-nerf.md) which relies on explicit deformation fields, it uses semantic embeddings to guide this stratification.
+Gear-NeRF stratifies a 4D scene into discrete "gear" levels based on semantic motion scales, assigning higher-resolution feature volumes to regions with larger motion [13848-gear-nerf.md]. It differs from [NeRF with Time Parameter](14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md) by using discrete levels to allocate computational resources proportional to motion intensity, and from [KFD-NeRF](15551-kfd-nerf.md) by using semantic embeddings rather than explicit deformation fields [13848-gear-nerf.md]. Choose this method when motion varies significantly across different semantic regions of the scene.
 
 ### [NeRF with Time Parameter](14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md)
-Adapts the standard NeRF architecture by introducing a temporal coordinate $t$ as an additional input to the neural network. It solves the problem of static scene representation in video sequences by allowing the density and color fields to vary over time. Unlike sibling methods that use explicit deformation fields or particle systems, this approach relies solely on input expansion, treating time as a direct coordinate alongside spatial position and view direction. While it improves reconstruction quality over vanilla NeRF for dynamic content, it is limited by its inability to explicitly model scene dynamics, often resulting in blurry predictions and ghosting artifacts.
+This method adapts standard NeRF by introducing $t$ as a direct input to the MLP, allowing density and color to vary over time [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. It relies solely on input expansion without explicit deformation fields [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. It is the simplest baseline for dynamic scenes but suffers from blurriness and ghosting artifacts due to the lack of explicit motion modeling [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md].
 
 ### [Forward Warping for Novel View Synthesis](15541-forward-warping-for-novel-view-synthesis.md)
-Renders dynamic scenes by modeling a static scene at a canonical time step and forward warping the entire canonical radiance field to other time steps. It addresses the many-to-one and one-to-many mapping difficulties inherent in dynamic scene reconstruction by employing differentiable forward warping with average splatting and an inpaint network. Unlike backward flow methods such as NDVG, this approach leverages a canonical representation to simplify the deformation field learning, achieving state-of-the-art performance on public datasets.
+This method models a static scene at a canonical time step and forward warps the radiance field to other times using a deformation field and differentiable splatting [15541-forward-warping-for-novel-view-synthesis.md]. It addresses many-to-one mapping difficulties inherent in dynamic reconstruction [15541-forward-warping-for-novel-view-synthesis.md]. It differs from backward flow methods by leveraging a canonical representation, achieving state-of-the-art performance on public datasets [15541-forward-warping-for-novel-view-synthesis.md].
 
 ### [Dynamic Appearance Particle Neural Radiance Field](15550-dynamic-appearance-particle-neural-radiance-field.md)
-Models dynamic scenes from monocular video data by integrating a particle-based motion network with a static grid-based radiance field. It solves the problem of reconstructing temporally varying scenes without requiring multi-view stereo data at each time step, which is a constraint of related particle-based methods like ParticleNeRF. Unlike ParticleNeRF, which encodes the entire scene with particles and relies on radius nearest neighbor searching, this method applies appearance particles only to dynamic elements and uses a grid-based field with linear interpolation for efficient querying.
+This method integrates a particle-based motion network with a static grid-based radiance field to model dynamic scenes from monocular video [15550-dynamic-appearance-particle-neural-radiance-field.md]. It solves the multi-view constraint of methods like ParticleNeRF by applying appearance particles only to dynamic elements [15550-dynamic-appearance-particle-neural-radiance-field.md]. It differs from ParticleNeRF by using a grid-based field with linear interpolation for efficient querying [15550-dynamic-appearance-particle-neural-radiance-field.md].
 
 ### [KFD-NeRF](15551-kfd-nerf.md)
-Models the 4D radiance field as a dynamic system with temporally varying states, estimated using a Kalman filter to combine observations from input data and predictions based on local linear motion equations. It differs from sibling methods like [Gear-NeRF](13848-gear-nerf.md) or [NeRF with Time Parameter](14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md) by explicitly separating motion estimation into an observer branch (shallow MLP) and a predictor branch (linear motion model), fusing them via a learnable Kalman gain. Unlike feature-interpolation approaches, it uses deformation fields warped to a canonical space represented by tri-planes, enabling smoother learning and faster convergence.
+KFD-NeRF models the 4D radiance field as a dynamic system estimated using a Kalman filter, combining observations from input data with predictions from local linear motion equations [15551-kfd-nerf.md]. It explicitly separates motion estimation into an observer branch and a predictor branch, fusing them via a learnable Kalman gain [15551-kfd-nerf.md]. It uses deformation fields warped to a canonical space represented by tri-planes, enabling smoother learning than feature-interpolation approaches [15551-kfd-nerf.md].
 
 ## Background
-The standard NeRF architecture [Parent](3065-nerf-hyperparameters.md) serves as the baseline for most modifications. It consists of two Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs): a coarse network and a fine network. The coarse network takes positional encoded position $\gamma(\mathbf{x})$ and outputs a latent feature vector $\mathbf{h}$ and volume density $\sigma$. The fine network takes $\mathbf{h}$, positional encoded position $\gamma(\mathbf{x})$, and positional encoded view direction $\gamma(\mathbf{d})$ to output color $\mathbf{c}$ [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
+The standard NeRF architecture [Parent](3065-nerf-hyperparameters.md) was originally designed for static scenes. Extensions to dynamic scenes emerged to handle video data, with methods ranging from simple time-conditioned MLPs [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md] to complex deformation and particle-based systems [15541-forward-warping-for-novel-view-synthesis.md][15550-dynamic-appearance-particle-neural-radiance-field.md][15551-kfd-nerf.md]. These methods address the limitations of static representations in video sequences, such as ghosting and blurriness [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md].
 
-$$
-\gamma(\mathbf{x}, d_1, d_2) = [\mathbf{x}, \sin(2^{d_1}\pi\mathbf{x}), \cos(2^{d_1}\pi\mathbf{x}), \dots, \sin(2^{d_2-1}\pi\mathbf{x}), \cos(2^{d_2-1}\pi\mathbf{x})]
-$$
-
-Modifications generally fall into three categories [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md]:
-1.  **Input Expansion:** Adding new conditioning vectors (e.g., appearance embeddings $\mathbf{z}_i$, fluid volume $v$, or previous stage RGB) to the input concatenation [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
-2.  **Output Expansion:** Adding auxiliary heads to predict values other than $\sigma$ and $\mathbf{c}$, such as semantic labels $y_{aux}$, object codes $o$, or area encoding probabilities $p$. These are rendered using the same transmittance weights $T_i$ and opacity $\alpha_i$ as color:
-    $$
-    \hat{Y}(r) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} T_i \alpha_i y_{aux}(x_i)
-    $$
-    [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
-3.  **Representation Substitution:** Replacing the continuous MLP function with discrete structures (voxel grids, hash tables) or different implicit surfaces (SDFs) [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
